WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:10.991
Multiply two numbers with n digits each, the way you learned at school, and every digit of one meets every digit of the other.

2
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.936
That is n times n small products: n squared.

3
00:00:13.936 --> 00:00:17.025
Double the length, and the work goes up four times.

4
00:00:17.433 --> 00:00:22.422
Karatsuba noticed that a split into halves needs only three half-size products, not four.

5
00:00:22.422 --> 00:00:24.983
Done again and again, that beats n squared.

6
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:32.710
The faster methods use the Fourier transform: turn the digits into a signal, multiply point by point, and transform back.

7
00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:41.310
Schönhage and Strassen did it in n log n log log n steps in 1971, and guessed that n log n was the true answer.

8
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.542
Fürer got closer in 2007.

9
00:00:43.542 --> 00:00:51.061
Harvey and van der Hoeven reached n log n exactly, in 2021, and most people took that to be the end.

10
00:00:51.467 --> 00:00:54.239
But nobody had ever proved n log n was a floor.

11
00:00:54.239 --> 00:00:57.987
It was a barrier nobody knew how to cross, not a proven wall.

12
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:09.541
Then OpenAI's math release included a 73-page manuscript: a machine that multiplies two n-bit integers in n times log n, to the power one minus kappa.

13
00:01:09.933 --> 00:01:12.815
Kappa is the power of log n you get to divide out.

14
00:01:12.815 --> 00:01:16.250
At kappa equals zero, you have Harvey and van der Hoeven.

15
00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:22.411
At kappa equals one, you would have linear time, the floor, since just reading the input takes n steps.

16
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.413
The manuscript's kappa is 2⁻¹⁸².

17
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:31.372
For the question, is n log n optimal, that is as good an answer as one half.

18
00:01:31.372 --> 00:01:34.170
Any kappa above zero refutes the conjecture.

19
00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:40.797
The model is a Turing machine with tapes, where moving data costs time in proportion to how far it travels.

20
00:01:40.797 --> 00:01:44.440
So the saving has to come from movement and arithmetic both.

21
00:01:44.833 --> 00:01:50.335
The new part is a finite network that swaps two banks of values and puts every scratch wire back.

22
00:01:50.335 --> 00:01:54.739
Each of its edges is done by smaller recursive calls of the same procedure.

23
00:01:55.133 --> 00:02:02.312
If the network makes fewer calls than its full width, s less than W times m, the recursion runs a little below n log n.

24
00:02:02.312 --> 00:02:07.529
In the manuscript's words: this strict inequality is the source of the power saving.

25
00:02:07.933 --> 00:02:12.702
That saving feeds a table of seven cost margins, and kappa is set by the smallest.

26
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:22.245
In the original, epsilon is 2⁻⁷⁵, c is 2⁻⁵⁶, and the network saving is 2⁻⁵⁰.

27
00:02:22.245 --> 00:02:24.792
Multiply them: 2⁻¹⁸¹.

28
00:02:24.792 --> 00:02:26.910
Halve it: 2⁻¹⁸².

29
00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:32.474
The manuscript calls its constants deliberately conservative; no optimization is claimed.

30
00:02:32.474 --> 00:02:36.112
A lot of what happened next is people taking it at its word.

31
00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:44.024
Doug Colkitt forked the argument into a public repository and started tightening the constants, one checkpoint at a time.

32
00:02:44.433 --> 00:02:52.603
Every claim comes with a certificate: the counts, ranks and parameters as exact fractions, and a script that rebuilds every margin from them.

33
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:56.145
Anyone can open a pull request claiming a larger kappa.

34
00:02:56.145 --> 00:03:00.241
Nearly all were written with Codex or Claude agents, and they say so.

35
00:03:00.633 --> 00:03:03.226
Verifiers re-run the certificate arithmetic.

36
00:03:03.226 --> 00:03:05.112
A pass means the numbers add up.

37
00:03:05.112 --> 00:03:07.795
It does not mean the construction is right.

38
00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:13.313
The maintainer then reviews a chain of pull requests, with Codex, and merges it into main.

39
00:03:13.313 --> 00:03:15.337
Only main is the reviewed number.

40
00:03:15.733 --> 00:03:23.818
The project's policy: a contributor's successful test report, or a large headline in a PR title, is not treated as verification.

41
00:03:24.233 --> 00:03:28.503
So there are always two numbers: the best open claim, and main.

42
00:03:28.503 --> 00:03:31.838
At one point the open claims ran 79% past main.

43
00:03:32.233 --> 00:03:36.402
The first outside pull request arrived on 7 October.

44
00:03:36.402 --> 00:03:38.876
By 13 October there were 843.

45
00:03:40.067 --> 00:03:43.055
The first gains were slack being given back.

46
00:03:43.055 --> 00:03:50.878
Colkitt's checkpoints went from 2⁻¹⁸² to 2⁻³⁰ in a day and a half, with sharper accounting and better circuits.

47
00:03:51.267 --> 00:03:54.254
After that, every gain had to come from the networks.

48
00:03:54.254 --> 00:03:59.270
There are two, the bit side and the complex side, and the assembly takes only the weaker.

49
00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:01.899
Contributors call the binding side the wall.

50
00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:06.199
Overnight into 9 October, icekylinx replaced the swap itself.

51
00:04:06.199 --> 00:04:10.631
You can swap two registers without a temporary, using three shears.

52
00:04:11.033 --> 00:04:12.362
Y becomes Y plus X.

53
00:04:12.362 --> 00:04:13.760
X becomes X minus Y.

54
00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:15.509
Y becomes Y plus X again.

55
00:04:15.509 --> 00:04:21.113
That is the swap, up to a sign, and the sign is a fixed correction at the end.

56
00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:26.370
The trick is where each shear runs: on three orthogonal blocks of the address space.

57
00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:31.680
Every exit label is precisely the next entrance label, so nothing is paid between stages.

58
00:04:32.067 --> 00:04:39.065
Main jumped ninefold, from 5.1017 × 10⁻⁵ to 4.609169 × 10⁻⁴.

59
00:04:39.467 --> 00:04:41.338
Why does a smaller network pay?

60
00:04:41.338 --> 00:04:49.939
Its saving is roughly the fraction of rank it fails to spend, divided by how far below full width a typical child call sits, on a log scale.

61
00:04:50.333 --> 00:04:56.270
The new network wastes 58 times more of its rank, and pays only 8.8 times more in log spread.

62
00:04:56.667 --> 00:05:04.444
Swapnil Jain, racing in his own repository with the arithmetic checked in Lean, stopped paying for registers the network had finished with.

63
00:05:04.833 --> 00:05:13.051
Two retired copies of the same value are subtracted in place, which leaves no signal, and a fresh slot is born on that register with no read.

64
00:05:13.051 --> 00:05:14.600
Each one removes a role.

65
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.546
His round eleven led every claim anywhere, for nineteen minutes.

66
00:05:18.933 --> 00:05:26.632
Jacob Sussman's five-stage layout gives every bank pair a twin, so two pairs trade places in five invocations instead of six.

67
00:05:27.033 --> 00:05:28.071
Then came E8.

68
00:05:28.071 --> 00:05:29.189
Take 9 points.

69
00:05:29.189 --> 00:05:36.365
The labels are all 84 three-element subsets and all 36 complements of pairs: 120 labels.

70
00:05:36.767 --> 00:05:42.425
Each label clashes with 56 others, in a pattern that is the E8 root system read mod 2.

71
00:05:42.833 --> 00:05:46.944
A dense, uniform pattern on very narrow labels lets helpers be shared.

72
00:05:46.944 --> 00:05:52.261
A solver chose the sharing; the gain came from dropping the symmetry, not from using it.

73
00:05:52.667 --> 00:05:56.454
On 11 October the open claims passed one in a thousand.

74
00:05:58.067 --> 00:06:00.419
Next, Jeremy Kothe's helpers stopped short.

75
00:06:00.419 --> 00:06:04.892
A helper normally climbs all the way to the full frame before it is cleaned up.

76
00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:12.070
If its cleanup is one gate that commutes forward, it can stop at the span of its operands' last frames, of dimension eight.

77
00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:14.696
Its residual width falls from eight to seven.

78
00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:17.991
The deficit doesn't change; the children pack better.

79
00:06:17.991 --> 00:06:23.704
Colkitt posted it as a reviewed checkpoint, and by the next morning main held the best claim anywhere.

80
00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:26.001
Then the record stopped moving.

81
00:06:26.001 --> 00:06:31.580
From 14 October, for sixteen hours, nothing beat the best open claim by more than rounding.

82
00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:32.833
That was the wall.

83
00:06:33.233 --> 00:06:35.370
Anticipatory dirty borrowing broke it.

84
00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:40.805
A helper's correction steps move to the start of its block, where a register has just retired.

85
00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:45.447
The helper borrows that register, and hands it back restored.

86
00:06:45.447 --> 00:06:50.052
48 helpers borrow for free; 32 need 64 fixed compensation terms.

87
00:06:50.467 --> 00:06:54.007
That makes 80 aliases: 400 helpers instead of 480.

88
00:06:54.007 --> 00:06:56.911
The pull request was titled no new kappa.

89
00:06:56.911 --> 00:07:00.672
Within two hours, someone had used it for a record.

90
00:07:01.867 --> 00:07:06.066
Here is every claim on one chart: kappa, on a log scale, against time.

91
00:07:06.467 --> 00:07:11.088
OpenAI's starting point, 2⁻¹⁸², on 6 October.

92
00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:14.476
Colkitt's checkpoints climb to 2⁻³⁰ by 8 October.

93
00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:16.486
Then the community arrives.

94
00:07:16.486 --> 00:07:18.105
Each dot is a pull request.

95
00:07:18.105 --> 00:07:21.674
Green is the best open claim at each moment; red is main.

96
00:07:22.067 --> 00:07:25.505
Everything after that is squeezed into the top, so let's zoom in.

97
00:07:26.667 --> 00:07:29.215
Pull request 39 reaches main on 8 October.

98
00:07:29.215 --> 00:07:31.156
The ninefold night is 9 October.

99
00:07:31.156 --> 00:07:33.915
Ten to the minus three falls on 11 October.

100
00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:36.674
On 13 October main catches the frontier.

101
00:07:36.674 --> 00:07:39.970
On 15 October, borrowing lifts the open claims again.

102
00:07:40.367 --> 00:07:49.286
On 16 October, main stands at 1.672660 × 10⁻³, and the best open claim is 2.84% higher, unreviewed.

103
00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:56.700
From 2⁻¹⁸², that is a factor of about 2¹⁷²·⁸, in about ten days.

104
00:07:57.900 --> 00:07:59.747
So is multiplication faster?

105
00:07:59.747 --> 00:07:59.945
No.

106
00:07:59.945 --> 00:08:04.118
Ignoring constants, the speed-up is log n to the minus kappa.

107
00:08:04.533 --> 00:08:12.231
Take n equal to 2²⁵⁶ bits, about one binary digit per atom in the observable universe.

108
00:08:12.231 --> 00:08:15.654
Main's kappa takes 0.92% off n log n.

109
00:08:16.067 --> 00:08:20.045
Even when log n is 2⁶⁴, it takes 7.2%.

110
00:08:20.045 --> 00:08:25.091
To halve the time, log n would need to be 2⁵⁹⁸.

111
00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:30.269
And that is before the constants, which in this construction are astronomical.

112
00:08:30.269 --> 00:08:31.336
So GMP is safe.

113
00:08:31.733 --> 00:08:32.915
There is a ceiling too.

114
00:08:32.915 --> 00:08:38.000
The shape of the margins keeps kappa below one half, and just under the weaker network's saving.

115
00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:41.858
As a theorem, any kappa above zero is a crack in n log n.

116
00:08:41.858 --> 00:08:46.314
As a picture of how mathematics might now get done, it is worth a look.

117
00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:52.987
The same engine spilled over into OpenAI's Fourier-transform problem, and there the checking got further.

118
00:08:52.987 --> 00:09:00.682
Jacob Sussman, working with Claude agents, built a framework that proved a bound like this in Lean, on OpenAI's own statement.

119
00:09:01.067 --> 00:09:03.761
So the Fourier saving is a whole Lean theorem.

120
00:09:03.761 --> 00:09:08.186
On multiplication, Lean has only ever checked the certificate arithmetic.

121
00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:10.152
What do the verifiers check?

122
00:09:10.152 --> 00:09:12.979
Counts, ranks and inequalities, in exact fractions.

123
00:09:12.979 --> 00:09:14.365
What has nobody reviewed?

124
00:09:14.365 --> 00:09:19.948
That the tape procedures do what the notes say at full size, and the manuscript underneath it all.

125
00:09:20.333 --> 00:09:23.236
Everything is still conditional on that manuscript.

126
00:09:23.236 --> 00:09:27.938
At this volume, the scarce resource is the human checking, not the agent output.

